d291 strength, size, ability *aḷ-; *aḷ-av-; long-root alternative *āḷ? lāvu
Tamil aḷ strength and Kannada aḷa/aḷavu/aḷavi support short a and postvocalic lateral independently. Telugu alavi/alavu retain exactly the kind of low-vowel expansion compared in measure d295.
*aḷ-av > *ḷāv > Telugu lāvu, alongside retained alavi/alavu. Gondi lāv, Kolami la·v and Parji lāv could reflect the same historical development or spread of the Telugu adjective/noun; local donor diagnostics are needed.

d686 stroke, rub, anoint *uẓ-i/v-; long *ū-s anoint series uncertain duvvu
Malayalam uẓiyuka/uẓivu establishes vowel-first ẓ in the stroke family; Kannada ūḍu preserves vowel-first retroflex with long quantity. DEDR separates a stroke/scrape series from a long ū-s anoint series.
Conditional *uẓ-v > *ẓuvv > ḍuvv > duvvu gives Telugu stroke, sharing the comb formation in d689. *uẓ-u-s > *ẓūs could yield eastern ṛūs; an alternative *ūẓ-s > ūs accounts for vowel-first anoint forms by medial assimilation. ṛūs > ūs through later initial-r loss is another testable concealed-metathesis hypothesis, but requires independent evidence of that initial loss.

d1278 black, scorch, burn *kar-u; long *kār and consonantal/nasal derivatives krā̃gu
Tamil karu/karuku, Kannada karaku/karñ-type formations and Gondi kar support short kar independently, beside widespread long kār. Exact Telugu -aŋ formation is not supplied by bare karu.
Conditional *kar-aŋ > *krāŋ > krā̃gu; kaggu/kaŋgu may follow lost r or direct medial assimilation. Kui *kar-um > krum could preserve the formative u by syncope/assimilation, whereas grīpa needs high-vowel formation or other quality change.

d1287 strong, abundant, excessive *kar-u; Telugu consonantal/nasal derivative krandu-konu
Tamil karumai, Malayalam karu/karuttu and Kannada karu establish full kar order. Telugu karamu independently retains it.
Conditional *kar-nd > *kra-nd > krandu-konu fits short consonantal displacement; *kar-a-nd followed by contraction/shortening is an alternative without independent exact suffix evidence.

d1291 sound, call, ring *kar-ay; *kar-NK and labial formations krandu || kraŋgu
Tamil karai, Malayalam karayuka and Kannada kare independently support kar-a; Gondi karŋg supports consonantal/nasal formation without a full medial vowel.
Consonantal *kar-ŋg > *kra-ŋg fits Telugu kraŋgu; krandu has a distinct dental nasal extension. Conditional *kar-av > krāv gives Kui bell tongue, but its exact suffix is not independently matched.

d1292 melt, dissolve *kar-a-NK; *kar-k; high-vowel derivatives krā̃gu || krā̃cu
Kannada karaŋgu/karagu/kargu, Tamil karai and Telugu karãgu/karãcu independently provide full low and consonantal formations.
*kar-aŋg > *kra-aŋg > *krāŋg > krā̃gu and stronger transitive *kar-aŋkk > krā̃cu are plausible; exact transitive consonant and palatalization chronology must be separately reconstructed. Kuvi karangali retains the full low formation.

d1347 bonduc nut, molucca bean *kaẓ-aṟ/ñc; distinct from tuber *kiẓ-aŋk gracca
Malayalam kaẓañci/kaẓacci and the composite Tamil kaẓaŋku/kaẓal/kaẓaṟci row supply full apical order; Tulu kaḷeñji supplies a lateral variant. The tuber headword is a semantic mislink.
Conditional *kaẓ-a-cc > *kẓācc > gracca requires shortening/strengthening; an earlier consonantal *kaẓ-cc > *kẓacc gives short a directly. The exact suffix and botanical transmission are not settled.

d1349 loosen, slip off; move, shake *kaẓ-al; *kaẓ-i; nasal-velar derivatives krālu || krālugaṇṭi || krālgaṇṭi
Tamil/Malayalam/Kannada kaẓal and shorter kaẓi independently establish two apicals in the low-al formation.
*kaẓ-al > *kẓā-l > krālu. Gondi kaṛŋg retains initial order; Kui kṛeŋga/Pengo kṛaŋg are nasal-velar displaced formations with separate a/e development. Kui kṛepka additionally reverses stem-final k and suffix p.

d1359 backwater, salt marsh; canal *kaẓ-i; Telugu *kaẓ-y? krayya
Tamil and Malayalam kaẓi independently establish full apical order; Telugu canal kayya/krayya is semantically narrower.
Possible *kaẓ-y > *kẓa-y > krayya with strengthening; *kaẓ-iya with high-vowel loss is another route. kayya can follow r loss or direct ẓ/y assimilation.

d1368 full, spread, overflow *kaẓ-um; consonantal *kaẓ-m/cc krammu || kraccu
Tamil kaẓumu gives full apical and labial formation; Kui garja keeps a postvocalic rhotic but has a different palatal extension.
*kaẓ-m > *kẓam > krammu gives short vowel plus doubled labial; kraccu requires a different palatal formative. Syncope from *kaẓ-um before displacement is possible; final u is not automatically a contracting suffix.

d1477 vomit, spit, hawk *kār/ṟ/l; short Tulu *kar-āy derivative krāyu || grāyu
Tamil kāl/kāṟu, Kannada kāṟ and Kota/Kodagu long roots independently establish long quantity; Tulu karāya/karavɛ provide short-root expanded forms.
Possible *kar-āy > *krāy produces Telugu with aphaeresis/reassociation and suffixal long ā; alternatively *kār-y > krāy is long-vowel displacement. gāyu can then lose r, while initial k/g variation is separate.

d1507 Indian beech, Pongamia regional *kar-aŋ; tree compound *kar-aŋ#mar-an krānugu || kānugu || kānuga || kāngu || kāgu || gānuga
Konda kaṛaŋ maran:kṛāŋ mrānu supplies a directly matching full versus clustered compound; Tulu korŋgi/korŋgu supplies retained r.
*kar-aŋ > *krāŋ, with Telugu krānugu reflecting a further vowel/velar shape; later r loss gives kānugu/kānuga/kāngu. Tree maran>mrānu is d4711 and must not count as a second Pongamia-root event.

d1580 curd, thick buttermilk *ka/iẓ-al/ān krānu || kānu
Kannada kaẓal and Tamil kiẓāaṉ independently supply a medial approximant and a following low vowel, with distinct final l/n.
Conditional *kaẓ-an > *kẓān > krānu > kānu preserves final n. Tamil ki and long ā plus Kannada final l show that this exact immediate form is reconstructed, not simply copied from one modern cognate.

d1594 small, young, grow lean *kiṯ/ṟ-u/i/a; strong small compounds separate kriyyu
Kannada kiṟi/kiṟa/kiṟu and Tulu kiri/kiru independently establish the velar and short vowel; Tamil ciṟu reflects palatalization rather than an originally universal c.
Conditional *kiṟ-y > *kṟiy > kriyyu, with strengthening of the formative, or high-vowel syncope before movement. krēpu calf has a separate low/long formation or belongs to1123, so it is not a secure positive here.

d1767 bend, droop, sink low *kur-aŋk; *kur-k; long grade *kūr kruŋgu || kruŋgilu
Tamil kuraŋku and Kannada kurgu independently supply r and full/cluster formations; Kota koṛng also retains the medial apical.
Consonantal *kur-ŋg > kruŋgu; low-vowel *kur-aŋg > *kor-aŋg > krōŋga could yield Kui. Telugu kuŋgu could lose initial-cluster r or assimilate internally, as Kannada kuŋgu/kuggu does.

d1769 monkey *kur-aŋk; regional *kor-a-nt? krō̃ti || kō̃ti
Tamil kuraŋku, Malayalam kuraŋŋu, Kannada koraŋgi and Tulu kuraŋga independently establish a low-a nasal-velar noun.
Conditional *kor-ant > *krōnt > krō̃ti > kō̃ti; this is not direct transformation of Tamil kuraŋku into Telugu. A velar-to-dental suffix history or distinct formative must be justified.

d1774 voice, throat; sing, recite *kur-al; verb *kor-al-c krōlcu || krōlucu
Tamil/Malayalam kural and Kannada koral/koralcu independently establish both r and later l, including a close verbal extension.
*kur-al > *kor-al; *kor-al-c > *krōl-c > krōlcu, with krōlucu showing an additional medial vowel. Kannada korḷu is syncope without initial displacement, koḷḷu can assimilate r internally.

d1822 rot, become soft, decay *kuẓ-ay; *kuẓ-l? and other derivatives kruḷḷu || kruḷḷagincu || kruḷḷaginta
Tamil kuẓai, Malayalam kuẓayuka and Kannada koẓe independently support a medial approximant. Exact lateral formative behind Telugu ḷḷ is not supplied by these low-ay verbs.
Conditional *kuẓ-l > *kẓu-l > kruḷḷu, or a fuller derivative plus syncope, supplies a short consonantal route. kuḷḷu may lose cluster r or reflect direct medial assimilation.

d1850 pound, strike with fist *kuṯ-u / *kuṯ-ṯ; regional *gur-d gruddu
Tamil kuṟu:kuṟṟu, Kota kur:kut and Gondi kurkal independently establish an apical weak/strong family. Kannada gurdu provides a close r+d formation.
*kur-d > *krud > gruddu with initial/suffix voicing and strengthening is plausible; guddu can be direct rd assimilation or later cluster-r loss. Labial kummu/guppu formations must be analyzed separately.

d1923 stuff, cram *kūr/kuṟ; regional *kul-uk krukku || krukkuḍu
Tamil kūṟu-koḷ and Telugu kūru establish a long r-bearing formation; Malayalam kuṟummuka and Kannada kuluku supply short forms with different apicals/suffixes.
Conditional *kur-kk > krukku, with independent medial syncope possible; kūru is not mechanically shortened by the exchange alone. kukku can be direct assimilation or later r loss.

d2008 side, flank, behind regional *kēr/ker-i krē || krēva || krēgannu
Kui kēri and Brahui xēr establish long root, Parji keri a short full form; all place r after the vowel.
Possible *ker-a-v > krēva lacks independent a/v support; alternative *kēr-V > krē involves a long root. krēgannu is a side-eye compound, not an independent event.

d2010 mock, deride regional *kēr/kīr; extensions d/n/s krēḍincu || krēṇincu
Telugu kēru/kēraḍamu and Gondi kīrī establish full long r-bearing forms independently of eastern grēs.
Possible *kēr-s > *krēs > Kui grēspa/Kuvi greh; Telugu krēḍincu/krēṇincu requires a dental/nasal formation with contraction, long-vowel reordering or analogy. These alternatives are not dated.

d2356 cord, rope, twine *car-aṭ trāḍu
Tamil and Malayalam caraṭu independently establish low a between r and retroflex ṭ; Kota carḍ supports a consonantal variant.
*car-aṭ > *crāṭ > trāḍu includes contraction, cluster-initial c/s>t and medial voicing. Kolami tāḍ/Naikri tāṛ can lose r after a cluster or reflect Telugu borrowing.

d2360 slip, slide, occur; decrease *car-i/u; regional *sar-k / *jar-k sraggu
Tamil cari/carukku, Kannada sari/saraku/jarugu and Tulu sarki/jari independently support full r order and consonantal derivatives.
Conditional *sar-g > sraggu with suffix strengthening/voicing; saggu can follow r loss or direct rg assimilation. Full jarugu and sraggu need not have identical initial or formative histories.

d2687 shrink, shrivel, wrinkle *cur-uŋk / *cur-kk; regional s/k initial correspondences srukku
Tamil curuŋku/curukku, Malayalam curuŋŋuka and Kannada surku independently establish medial r and full/consonantal formations.
*cur-kk > *crukk > srukku, with initial c>s separate. Earlier *curu-kk can syncopate before displacement, whereas Kannada sukku/Malayalam cukkuka can assimilate medially without metathesis.

d3095 churn, produce fire by rubbing *tar-i; *tar-c / *tar-V-c traccu || traccana
Parji terip/terit independently gives full apical order; Telugu tari/tarucu/tarcu provides an internal full/consonantal series.
*tar-c > *tra-c > traccu with strengthening is plausible; tarucu could be a fuller ancestor or a separately vocalized derivative. Parji labial p differs from Telugu palatal c.

d3115 fire, embers, fire pit *taẓ; *taẓ-m / *taẓ-al trampi
Tamil taẓal and Gondi taṛm/taṛmi independently establish apical order and a labial ember formation; Parji taṛ supplies a full root.
*taẓ-m-p > *tẓa-mp > trampi, then possible r loss > tampi. Tamil taṇal/Kannada taṇalu involve medial nasalization/assimilation, not initial reversal. Kui *taẓ-av > dlāv is conditional on the root assignment.

d3122 dig, excavate, scratch regional *taẓ/taṛ; labial and palatal derivatives travvu || travvaṭincu || travvaṭamu || tavvu
Gondi taṛānā/tark and Kuvi taṛj independently retain full order; Kuvi taṛj:tṛaj gives a close internal formation contrast. Other branches have long tāṛ grades.
*taẓ-v > *tẓav > travvu > tavvu with labial strengthening and later r loss; Kuvi taṛj > tṛaj has the same directional relation with a palatal suffix. Konda ṟeb/Kuvi ṛev/Pengo ṛav can follow C1 loss, but e/o variants require distinct vowel histories.

d3174 drink, swallow regional *tār-g; earlier short formation unverified trāgu || trāvu || trāguḍu || trāvuḍu
Parji tār and Gadaba tārg independently supply full long-vowel order. Their transmission history is not established by the dictionary grouping.
Possible *tār-g > trāg is long-vowel displacement; alternatively a short vowel-bearing derivative first contracted, or central full forms arose by adaptation of a loan. Telugu trāvu has medial velar weakening and nouns add morphology.

d3192 wither, become lean *tāṟ; short *taṟ-al/agu and consonantal derivatives trāḍuvaḍu
Malayalam tāṟuka, Kannada tāṟ and Telugu tāṟu establish a long root. Kannada taṟal/taṟagu and Tulu taruṇṭu independently establish short expanded forms.
Possible *taṟ-aḍ > trāḍ followed by paḍu compound, or long *tāṟ-ḍ with reordering. No full matching aḍ derivative is supplied, and the short grade cannot be chosen merely to fit the output.

d3340 push, drive, propel *tur-a; labial and other derivatives trōyu || trōcu || trōlu || trōpu || trōpuḍu || drobbu || tōlu
Tamil tura/turattu, Malayalam turattuka and Parji turkip support full t-V-r independently of Telugu trō-.
Conditional *tor-a-y/c/l > trōy/c/l, with low-a root supported but exact suffixes distinct. *tor-b > trobb/drobb gives a short voiced-geminate derivative; dobbu can be later r loss or imported/assimilated form. Gondi ro- can lose C1 after a cluster.

d3343 rust, verdigris *tur-u; labial/velar strong formations truppu || trukku
Tamil turu and Malayalam turuvu/turumpu independently establish a rhotic root and a labial extension.
Possible *tur-pp > truppu and *tur-kk > trukku; full *tur-upp/ukk plus syncope is an alternative. Telugu tuppu may be later r loss or an independently assimilated form.

d3437 snap, break, cut *te/tiṟ-i; velar/labial/nasal derivatives treyyu || tregu || trevvu || trempu || trencu
Tamil teṟi, Malayalam teṟikka and Kannada tiṟi independently establish full apical order and a high-i base.
Consonantal *teṟ-g/v/mp/nc > treg/trevv/tremp/trenc with strengthening and nasal distinctions. Earlier high-i deletion is possible. tegu/tempu/tencu can subsequently lose r but may also converge with direct medial assimilation.

d3522 tread, trample, crush *tuẓ-i; velar/labial derivatives trokku || trokkuḍu
Kannada tuẓi, Malayalam toẓikka and Kuvi tuṛb independently establish medial apical order and distinct consonantal formations.
Conditional *toẓ-k > *tẓok > trokku gives short o and strengthened k. If earlier *tuẓ-ik is used, vowel lowering/reassociation must be explained separately; do not infer low a from o.

d3962 split, tear, slice *par-i; consonantal *par-t/d and velar/labial derivatives bradda || braddara
Tamil pari, Tulu partɛ and Kolami part establish full/cluster apical order, with a close dental tear formation. The source explicitly warns r/ṟ semantic convergence with4027.
Possible *par-d > *prad > bradda with voicing/strengthening; badda may lose cluster r or assimilate internally. Kui *par-a-k > prāk followed by k+p > pk yields pṛāpka, while parpa retains order.

d3972 grow large, increase, flourish *par-u; *par-b/d; long *pār prabbu
Tamil paru/paruppu and Malayalam paru independently establish short par; Kannada hariba/bardu and Kurux pard support labial/dental expansions.
Possible *par-b > *prab > prabbu, with strengthened b; pambu/pammu may be separate labial/nasal assimilation routes. Kannada bardu is dental, hariba labial, so exact Telugu b antiquity needs reconstruction.

d3999 old, worn, stale *paẓ-a; *paẓ-a-nt/n prā̃ta || prā || prā̃baḍu || prā̃tagillu || pā̃ta || pāta
Tamil/Malayalam/Kannada paẓa and Gondi paṛana independently establish low-a full input; Gondi paṛna also supplies a syncopated control.
*paẓ-ant > *pẓānt > prā̃ta > pā̃ta/pāta; *paẓ-an > prān in Pengo/Manda. Kuvi prāi > pṇāi may assimilate the promoted liquid to a nasal, but reverse order of assimilation/movement is also possible.

d4002 blame, scold, censure *paẓ-i; consonantal y/b derivatives prayi || prabbu
Tamil/Malayalam/Kannada paẓi independently establish the same high-i full input; Kurux paṛᵃgnā retains full apical order.
Conditional *paẓ-y > prayi and *paẓ-b > prabbu, or high-i deletion before consonantal formation; strengthening/voicing in bb is additional. The short a does not independently establish that V2 had already disappeared.

d4175 rattan, cane *pir-amp; strengthened labial variant prēmu || prabba || prabbili || pēmu
Tamil pirampu and Malayalam perampu independently establish full r order and low-a labial extension.
*pir-amp > *priamp > prēmu, with coalescence and nasal/labial simplification; prabba/prabbili require a strengthened short-vowel formation, not this exact sequence. pēmu can follow r loss.

d4176 separate, split, loosen *pir-i; *pir-k / nasal and labial derivatives pridulu
Tamil piṟi, Malayalam piriyuka, Kannada hiri and Brahui pirɣẖing establish full r order independently.
*pir-k > prik in Kuvi; Telugu *pir-id-ul or *pir-d-ul > pridulu remains a formation hypothesis. Kui prīva can reflect same-i coalescence, priŋg a consonantal derivative.

d4193 intestine, bowel *piẓ-ul / *piẓ-uv-ul; Telugu velar derivative prēgu || pēgu
Parji piṛul/piṛuvul, Gadaba puṛug and Gondi pīr give independent medial apical order, with divergent vowels and suffixes.
Conditional *piẓ-V-g > *pẓiVg > prēgu > pēgu; low-a coalescence is not independently supported here. Southern-central apical-to-velar/stop developments and noun morphology must be distinguished.

d4283 protect, nourish, support *pur-a; palatal/labial/dental derivatives prōcu || prōdi || prōpu || prōpaḍu || prāpu
Tamil pura/puravu and Kannada pore support low-vowel full order independently; Gondi porp and Parji porip add full consonantal/high-i formations.
Conditional *por-a-c/d/p > prōc/d/p with coalescence; exact suffixes differ. prāpu requires an a-grade, later vowel change or analogical influence, not the same o+a contraction.

d4312 worm, decay *puẓ-u; labial/velar and plural formations pruvvu || pruccu || pruppi || pruŋguḍu
Tamil/Malayalam puẓu, Kannada puẓu, Konda piṛvu and Telugu puruvu/purvu independently support full order and both vowel-bearing and consonantal branches.
*puẓ-v > pruvvu after cluster reordering/strengthening; alternative *puẓ-uv > *pẓuuv with later shortening cannot be excluded. Kui piṛu > pṛiu > pṛīu provides a high-vowel displacement/coalescence comparison; Kuvi pliguli includes lateral outcome and separate morphology.

d4537 parch, fry; hearth *por-i; y/d/n/s formations proyi || proyyi || poyi || poyyi
Tamil pori, Kannada puri, Tulu poriyuni and Gondi poṛs independently establish full r order.
*por-y > proyyi with strengthening; possible high-i loss before this stage. poyyi may lose r, while Kannada burugalu and Telugu borugulu preserve medial r in a separate grain noun. Eastern long brō/brū forms have different dental/nasal morphology.

d4545 earring *por-c; Telugu nasal-velar formation prō̃gu || pō̃gu
Parji porca and Gondi porskeng independently supply medial r in earring names.
Conditional *por-Vŋg > prō̃gu > pō̃gu; *por-c cannot directly yield the long vowel and nasal velar without additional morphology or sound changes.

d4559 sun, day, time *poẓ-ut; *poẓ-t; deictic compounds proddu || poddu
Tamil poẓutu, Kannada poẓtu/portu and Gondi poṛdu independently establish full and consonantal orders. The database ap-pōẓ head is a compound, not the ancestor of every member.
*poẓ-t > *pẓot > proddu with stop strengthening/voicing; high-u syncope may precede exchange. Telugu poddu may lose r; eastern pod forms could instead assimilate directly. appuḍu/ippuḍu/eppuḍu have deictic boundaries and a separate reduction history.

d4591 heap *pōr; short *por-aḷ formation prōgu || prōka
Tamil/Malayalam pōr and Konda pōri give independent long root; Kannada poraḷi gives a short expanded grade.
Possible *pōr-k > prōk or short *por-a-k > prōk with contraction. The independently attested short derivative ends in ḷ, not k, so exact contraction remains conditional.

d4599 split into threads; strand *pōẓ; nasal-velar/palatal derivatives prō̃gu || prō̃ju || pō̃gu || pō̃ju
Tamil pōẓ, Kannada pōẓ and Parji pōṛ establish a long full apical root independently.
Possible *pōẓ-ŋg/j > prōŋg/j with displacement; an unrecorded short expanded allomorph is another hypothesis, not an independently reconstructed fact. pō̃gu/pō̃ju can follow r loss.

d4728 darkness, cloud, stupor *mar-b / *mar-v mrabbu
Kannada marbu/marvu and Tulu marbu independently establish a medial r-labial cluster, beside assimilated mabbu.
*mar-b > mrabb with reordering and strengthening; mabbu can follow either mrabbu r loss or direct marbu assimilation. Gondi mabbu explicitly comes from Telugu.

d4971 rough, knotty; tree stump *mur-aṭ; other roughness derivatives mrōḍu
Tamil muraṭu, Malayalam muraṭu and Kannada moraḍu independently establish a full low-a dental formation; Telugu moraḍu retains the same order.
*mor-aḍ > mrōḍ by displacement/coalescence. Kui mrōjo adds dental-palatal correspondence. Nasal moṇḍi can reflect a separate assimilated derivative and is not proof of a previous mr stage.

d4975 break, perish, kill *mur-i; velar/nasal derivatives and long grade bruŋgu || bruncu || bruyyu
Tamil muri/murukku/muruŋku and central murk independently establish full short-vowel order; Malayalam mūruka is a long grade, not evidence all inputs were long.
*mur-k > mruk, then Kui mruk-p > mrupk in the separate suffixal process. Telugu *mur-ŋg > *mruŋg > bruŋgu; *mur-y > bruyyu involves labial denasalization/strengthening. Full muruŋku leaves syncope versus later shortening open.

d5007 spoil, become mouldy; dirt *muṟ-i; *muṟ-k/g mrukku || mrukka || mukku || mukka
Tamil muṟi, Telugu muṟiki/muṟugu, Gondi murk and Tulu murguni independently support full apical order and consonantal variants.
*muṟ-k > mrukk with reordering/strengthening; *muṟ-ik > *muṟk by syncope is possible. mrukku > mukku is supported by the pair, while muggu could arise directly as in Kannada muggu.

d5123 bow, kneel, salute *moẓ-k/g; other apical derivatives mrokku || mroggu
Kannada moẓgu, Gondi moṛk and Parji moṛk independently establish a medial apical-velar cluster.
*moẓ-k/g > mrokk/mrogg with reordering and strengthening; Telugu mokku/moggu can be later r loss or direct assimilation. Kui mrōnga has a nasal-velar formation and unexplained long vowel relative to a short cluster input.

d5372 live, flourish, livelihood *wāẓ; short *waẓ-d-uk / regional *bar-d-uk braduku || bratuku || bradimi || bratikincu
Tamil vāẓ and Kannada bāẓ establish long root; Kannada barduku/barduŋku and baẓduŋku independently support a short expanded apical-dental formation.
*bar-d-uk > bra-d-uk, with initial w>b and apical development preceding or independent of the observed order change; bratuku has dental voicing variation. baduku/batuku can be direct apical loss or later cluster loss.

